[00:00] [scene:intro]
VISUAL: A woman is reading a book with a white, inflatable, whale-shaped robot floating beside her. People are interacting with a floating robot shaped like a whale in a dark room.
AUDIO: 'This soft flying robot companion is lighter than air and made to do things that most other robots struggle with. Inspired by floating friends like Tinkerbell, Studio Ghibli Soot Sprites, and Pokémon, a team of researchers have set out to explore what a floating robot'

[00:12] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A woman is looking at her phone while a white, inflatable, whale-shaped robot floats nearby.
AUDIO: 'might be good for, how humans might interact with them, and what advantages this type of robot might have over others. To start, soft floating robots are much quieter than drones with their spinning propellers, which means they\'re'

[00:24] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Jesse Orrall, CNET Senior Producer, is speaking to the camera.
AUDIO: 'less stressful to sound sensitive pets and people like me. They also create less of a disturbance in the air, allowing for more safe and gentle interaction with their surroundings. Human robot interaction is actually one'

[00:36] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: Two humanoid robots are shown in an industrial setting, with text "END-TO-END Autonomous Fleet".
AUDIO: 'of the biggest challenges facing the robot industry right now. Most robots being made for industrial workplaces are quite heavy, as industrial machines often are, which can create risk of injury to their human co-workers. Companies are working hard to create'

[00:49] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: A person is interacting with a bipedal robot (Digit from Agility Robotics) in a warehouse setting.
AUDIO: 'safer human robot interaction. But for now, robots working autonomously are frequently separated from human beings by a physical barrier. And robots that interact with people are often remotec controlled for safety reasons. Floating'

[01:02] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Jesse Orrall is speaking to the camera.
AUDIO: 'robots offer a solution by making the robot body itself soft and light, which may create opportunities for safe interaction between man and machine, even if the floating robot is operating autonomously. One proof of concept'

[01:15] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A white, inflatable, whale-shaped robot gently "boops" a person sleeping in bed.
AUDIO: 'researchers showed demonstrating this potential was the soft robot acting like a physical alarm clock, waking somebody up with a gentle boop. This brings me to another area where soft floating robots may have an advantage, creating an'

[01:28] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Jesse Orrall is speaking to the camera. A person is petting a robotic dog named Jennie.
AUDIO: 'emotional connection. There are several companies currently developing robots that are meant to make an emotional connection with the human beings that they work with. They range from full-size standing humanoids with facial'

[01:40] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Jesse Orrall is speaking to the camera.
AUDIO: 'movements to robot puppies for Alzheimer\'s and dementia patients who may not be able to care for a dog of their own. Lots of effort goes into making expressive or cute-l lookinging faces, which can sometimes veer'

[01:52] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: A close-up of a realistic humanoid robot face (Ameca).
AUDIO: 'unintentionally into the infamous uncanny valley where something not quite humanlooking creates intense feelings of discomfort. Another alternative is simply putting chat bots or AI into the machines to give them a sense of'

[02:05] [scene:comparison]
VISUAL: A hand gently touches the face of the humanoid robot Ameca.
AUDIO: 'aliveness. But if you\'ve ever tried talking to a chatbot for an extended period of time, that doesn\'t always last. What\'s interesting to me about these soft floating robots is that it may prove to be a less is more situation. Human beings are wired to'

[02:18] [scene:speaker]
VISUAL: Jesse Orrall is speaking to the camera.
AUDIO: 'project human feelings onto nonhumans, including inanimate objects. Common phrases like, "My printer doesn\'t like me today," or "My phone is dead," reveal this tendency in us. But this nearly'

[02:30] [scene:demo]
VISUAL: A woman is sitting on a couch, interacting with a floating whale-shaped robot.
AUDIO: 'featureless floating white balloon with fins does seem to evoke the shape of a whale, which may be enough to create an emotional bond with the right movements. Speaking, walking, and facial expressions are all areas where our'

